# git-wrong-email rules: which emails may author and commit in a repository,
# decided by the directory the repository lives in.
#
#   <directory>  <email> [<email> ...]
#
# The first directory that contains the repository wins, so specific rules go
# first and "*" -- everything else -- goes last. A repository under no listed
# directory and with no "*" rule is refused outright, on purpose: an unmatched
# repository is one you have not thought about yet.
#
# These should agree with whatever sets your identity in the first place
# (includeIf blocks in ~/.gitconfig, a per-repo user.email, a switcher tool).
# This file is the check, not the source -- it catches the case where the thing
# that was supposed to set your identity didn't.

~/work/acme/        you@acme.example
~/work/globex/      you@globex.example

# A client's work kept outside the directory named after them.
~/projects/side-contract/   you@globex.example

# Personal projects, listed explicitly rather than left to the "*" below --
# the global default is exactly the value a GUI client rewrites when it
# "syncs" your profile, so being explicit here is what makes the check bite.
~/code/             you@personal.example  you@users.noreply.github.com

*                   you@personal.example  you@users.noreply.github.com
